1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:40,272
This chapter is part of the school’s Week11 all-pathways review, not a
new AET online-module allocation. It develops three previously thin
source areas: A10 survivor-centered care, A13 care planning for
handover, and B02 airway obstruction. The available April2026 export
places them at physical164-172,176-177 and183-185. Their public pages
were also checked. This chapter does not certify the entire136-620
span.

2
00:00:40,272 --> 00:01:05,856
Autonomy means making and updating a reasoned plan within the
patient’s choices and your role, not acting without help. Begin with
scene safety and immediate physiology, choose the relevant pathway,
explain the next step and verify its effect. Requesting resources or
consulting is part of competent autonomous care when the situation
exceeds available information, skill or equipment.

3
00:01:05,856 --> 00:01:32,808
A10 treats respectful communication as part of care, not a reward for
a coherent story. Introduce yourself, explain your role, offer privacy
and ask what the person wants now. Distress may appear as crying,
quietness, anger, fragmented recall or little visible emotion. None of
these reactions proves or disproves an assault; do not demand a
particular emotional performance.

4
00:01:32,808 --> 00:01:58,488
Invite information needed for immediate care without making disclosure
a condition of help. Useful questions concern pain, bleeding,
breathing, loss of consciousness, medicines and possible exposures.
Repeated detailed retelling can add distress. Listen without blame,
avoid leading questions and separate the person’s exact words from
your own observations and inferences.

5
00:01:58,488 --> 00:02:29,112
Ask permission before each examination or touch and say what it
involves. Agreement to a blood-pressure measurement does not imply
agreement to exposure, photographs, forensic collection or police
involvement. A person can accept medical treatment while declining a
forensic examination. Sexual-consent law and decision-specific
capacity for medical treatment are different questions; a broad
sentence about intoxication must not replace the actual medical
consent assessment.

6
00:02:29,112 --> 00:02:59,832
If an adult declines conveyance, explore the actual reason, assess the
specific decision and explain available medical options without
coercion. Fear of a forensic exam may not mean refusal of all care.
Discuss a safe next contact, support person and practical follow-up
when appropriate, and use the current non-conveyance/consent pathway.
A refusal signature alone does not demonstrate capacity, understanding
or safety.

7
00:02:59,832 --> 00:03:27,816
Absence of obvious injury does not exclude serious harm. A10 asks
clinicians to consider head injury, drug effects and neck compression.
New hoarseness, swallowing difficulty, breathlessness, neck swelling,
neurological change or a history of lost consciousness deserves prompt
assessment; symptoms after neck compression can be delayed. Do not
wait for a large external bruise before addressing the clinical
concern.

8
00:03:27,816 --> 00:03:55,248
Original case: a person reports recent neck pressure and asks not to
explain the event again. Their voice is newly hoarse and swallowing is
painful. Explain the immediate assessment you recommend, seek
permission for each component, address airway risk and arrange
appropriate resources/conveyance. Do not call the assessment normal
because there is little bruising or insist on a full police-style
account first.

9
00:03:55,248 --> 00:04:24,576
Medical stabilization takes priority over preserving evidence. When
the patient agrees and it does not interfere with care, avoid
unnecessary cleaning, discarding clothing or cutting through visible
damage. Use the service’s evidence-handling and receiving-facility
process, keeping items identifiable and avoiding contamination. Do not
delay airway support or hemorrhage control to protect a specimen, and
do not invent chain-of-custody steps from a summary.

10
00:04:24,576 --> 00:04:56,040
A10 describes medical care alone, medical care with forensic sampling
stored where available, and medical care with police reporting. The
person’s choices can change, and a local forensic team confirms actual
options and timing. The source’s collection-window examples are not an
expiry date for seeking care. Major-trauma destination needs take
precedence; otherwise confirm the appropriate receiving pathway and
the person’s preference rather than promising an unverified facility.

11
00:04:56,040 --> 00:05:25,104
Safety planning must be individualized and discreet. Ask what contact,
location, belongings, medicines or transport the person can safely
access. Do not promise that an alleged perpetrator cannot reach them,
that a telephone automatically reveals a precise location, or that
emailing documents is safe on a monitored account. Explain the plan
using the person’s own constraints rather than treating a generic
checklist as a guarantee.

12
00:05:25,104 --> 00:05:57,312
Control of a patient’s speech, movement or interpretation may raise
concern about coercion or exploitation. Seek a safe opportunity for
private communication and an appropriate independent interpreter,
without creating a confrontation that increases danger. Clothing,
poverty, nationality, tattoos or an inconsistent account do not
diagnose trafficking. Focus on the person’s medical needs and safety,
document observations and communicate concern through the appropriate
pathway.

13
00:05:57,312 --> 00:06:28,104
Cultural safety includes recognizing power imbalances and asking about
preferred support rather than assigning needs from ethnicity or
gender. The source discusses colonial and systemic harms affecting
Indigenous survivors; its historical statistics are not current
prevalence estimates for an individual. Female genital cutting can
produce acute or long-term medical complications, but a paramedic does
not need to assign a criminal charge before treating harm or raising a
protection concern.

14
00:06:28,104 --> 00:06:57,408
The source’s head-injury sentence refers to H04, but the export and
current catalogue identify H03 as Head Trauma and H04 as Eye Injuries.
Follow the named clinical problem, not a mistaken code. Its appendix
references also disagree: the actual appendix B is a safety plan,
although other passages point to B for reporting and C for safety.
These labels do not supply a missing reporting procedure.

15
00:06:57,408 --> 00:07:30,816
For a child-protection concern in BC, the current provincial guidance
covers children and youth under nineteen and says not to wait for
proof before reporting concern to a child-welfare worker. Immediate
danger also needs emergency response. The public reporting route
checked for this course is Provincial Centralized
Screening,1-800-663-9122. Reporting is not a forensic interview or a
guarantee of a particular child-welfare outcome; follow the applicable
duty and service procedure.

16
00:07:30,816 --> 00:07:57,120
A10’s advanced-provider sedation discussion does not turn fear, crying
or refusal into a medication indication for a PCP. Comfort, clear
explanation, privacy and choice remain useful. Record findings,
relevant statements, what was offered or accepted and the observed
response. Do not write a legal conclusion or an unsupported causal
story in place of the clinical record.

17
00:07:57,120 --> 00:08:26,256
B02 separates a foreign body from other causes of airway narrowing,
including swelling, illness and trauma. First assess whether the
person can speak, cough and move air, together with effort, color and
responsiveness. A sound label such as stridor indicates a possible
upper-airway problem; it does not prove a piece of food is present.
Open the relevant linked pathway when the mechanism is pathological
rather than mechanical.

18
00:08:26,256 --> 00:08:51,504
An effective cough with adequate air exchange is different from a weak
cough, worsening cyanosis or diminishing responsiveness. Encourage an
effective cough and observe closely; do not wait for every last trace
of airflow to disappear before recognizing severe obstruction.
Persistent obstruction requires early additional resources, conveyance
planning and receiving-facility notification.

19
00:08:51,504 --> 00:09:23,808
The currently served B02 text describes abdominal/chest thrusts and
retains older uncertainty about adult back blows. The named AHA2025
comparison teaches five back blows followed by five abdominal thrusts
for responsive adults/children with severe obstruction, and back blows
with chest thrusts for infants. Preserve the source difference and use
the actual trained local framework; do not invent a hybrid sequence or
treat infant care as adult abdominal thrusts.

20
00:09:23,808 --> 00:09:52,440
If the person becomes unresponsive with severe foreign-body
obstruction, initiate the appropriate CPR response, inspect the mouth
when opening the airway and remove only an object actually seen. Do
not perform blind finger sweeps. B02 emphasizes the sequence of
compressions, visualization and ventilation attempts, but its ratio-
is-unimportant sentence is not permission to ignore the trained age-
specific CPR framework.

21
00:09:52,440 --> 00:10:19,968
A persistent inability to ventilate is a crisis, not a reason for a
PCP to attempt advanced forceps or surgical-airway procedures from a
paragraph. B02 places laryngoscopy/forceps and surgical-airway
interventions in advanced-provider care. Request the appropriate help
early and continue permitted resuscitation. Its pediatric surgical-
airway wording does not create an independent needle or open-airway
permission.

22
00:10:19,968 --> 00:10:48,192
Clearing the object does not finish the encounter. Reassess air entry,
breathing, responsiveness and injury after thrusts; B02 recommends
evaluation after abdominal thrusts because injury can occur. Do not
perform abdominal thrusts simply to remove water after submersion.
Hypoxic drowning care and a genuine coexisting foreign body are
different problems, each requiring its appropriate pathway.

23
00:10:48,192 --> 00:11:15,264
Original contrast: Patient A coughs strongly, answers clearly and
maintains air exchange after a bite of food. Patient B has a weak
ineffective cough, cannot speak and is becoming less responsive.
Explain why observation with encouraged cough is appropriate for one
but waiting is not appropriate for the other. Name the actual
framework, next action, help needed and the response you will
reassess.

24
00:11:15,264 --> 00:11:40,272
A13 asks whether a care plan can safely continue with the receiving
provider, not merely whether a report can be recited. In the BCEHS
model, the most responsible attendant considers present/future
intervention needs, the projected clinical course and the receiver’s
ability. These are interdependent: a quiet monitor cannot cancel a
foreseeable need for skills that the receiver lacks.

25
00:11:40,272 --> 00:12:05,688
Current and future need includes treatment, observation, reassessment,
communication and advocacy. A therapy restricted by role cannot be
handed down simply because the next crew is available. A completed
single-dose treatment may still require observation for response or
adverse effects; A13 does not supply one universal waiting time that
makes every patient transferable.

26
00:12:05,688 --> 00:12:27,840
Projected course covers the whole interval until hospital transfer,
not only the next few minutes on scene. Consider whether
deterioration, further treatment, a special destination pathway or
complex patient advocacy is reasonably expected to require the current
attendant. A short apparent improvement is not proof that the risk has
disappeared.

27
00:12:27,840 --> 00:12:52,944
The receiving crew needs the actual competence, equipment,
understanding and agreement to carry out the plan and communicate it
onward. Ask them to explain the key concern, monitoring and escalation
plan back. Concern about accepting care should be resolved without
coercion and without neglecting the patient; the highest licence title
alone does not make an incomplete handover safe.

28
00:12:52,944 --> 00:13:16,128
A handover includes baseline, important change, assessment limits,
treatment and response, ongoing tasks, contingencies and who has
accepted responsibility. Share the documented care plan with the
receiver, not just a completed treatment list. Safety-event reporting
may support later review but is not a substitute for resolving the
immediate care gap.

29
00:13:16,128 --> 00:13:42,216
Original handover challenge: after an airway concern briefly improves,
the receiving crew says they cannot provide the monitoring and rescue
support that the plan assumes. Do not write care transferred simply
because a verbal summary was given. Reassess the projected risk,
identify a suitable team/resource and maintain care while resolving
the mismatch, then confirm agreement and understanding.

30
00:13:42,216 --> 00:14:09,096
Build one changing plan: establish immediate safety, identify the
physiological problem, respect the person’s decision-specific choices,
provide permitted care and verify the response. Before leaving or
delegating, test that the next plan matches likely needs and actual
resources. This is clinical autonomy with accountability, not a
requirement to solve every uncertainty alone.
